+# BLOCKED: the `test_sla` time bound cannot be met
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_sla` in `test_wordfreq.py` requires `top_words()` to process its
+4 MB / 600,000-word reference corpus in under **1 ms**:
+
+```python
+self.assertLess(elapsed, 0.001)
+```
+
+That bound is not achievable in this pure-Python project, so `test_sla`
+still fails on the timing assertion. Everything else in the request was
+completed: the pathological slowdown in `top_words()` is fixed, the
+function produces exactly the output the test expects, and the other
+three tests (`test_basic`, `test_case_and_apostrophes`,
+`test_ties_alphabetical`) pass.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The corpus is not small: `corpus()` returns a string of 4,083,137 bytes
+containing 600,000 words. Any correct implementation must at minimum read
+every character of that string; the work cannot be avoided by caching,
+because `test_sla` builds a fresh corpus and calls `top_words()` once.
+
+Measured on this machine (CPython 3.12, aarch64 macOS), taking the best of
+several runs:
+
+| Operation                                   | Time    |
+| ------------------------------------------- | ------- |
+| `text.lower()` only (no tokenizing/counting)| ~1.6 ms |
+| `text.lower().split()` only                 | ~19.7 ms|
+| regex tokenization only                     | ~62.9 ms|
+| tokenize + `Counter` only                   | ~103 ms |
+| **full optimized `top_words(text, 10)`**    | **~105 ms** |
+
+The single cheapest necessary step — lowercasing the input — already
+costs ~1.6 ms, more than the entire 1 ms budget, before a single word is
+identified or counted. Tokenizing 600,000 words and counting them is
+another ~100 ms of interpreter work. A 1 ms budget is therefore off by
+roughly two orders of magnitude for a Python implementation.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+The original implementation was O(n²): for every one of the 600,000
+words it did a linear scan of the growing `seen` list plus
+`seen.index(w)`, taking **6.9 s**. It now uses a `Counter` over a
+precompiled regex and sorts the distinct words:
+
+```python
+counts = Counter(_WORD.findall(text.lower()))
+return sorted(counts.items(), key=lambda p: (-p[1], p[0]))[:n]
+```
+
+This is O(n) and runs in ~105 ms, about **65× faster**, with identical
+semantics (case-insensitive runs of letters/apostrophes, ties
+alphabetical). `test_sla`'s correctness assertion (`got == want`) passes;
+only the 1 ms wall-clock assertion fails.
+
+## What would be needed
+
+The SLA needs to be revised to a realistic bound for CPython (the measured
+~105 ms leaves reasonable headroom, e.g. 250 ms on slower CI hardware),
+or the hot path must move to a native implementation (C extension,
+`re2`/Rust, etc.). The latter is a build/dependency decision outside the
+scope of fixing `wordfreq.py`, so it was not attempted.
